# The Retinue's Restlessness
Tamil title: பரிவாரத்தின் பதட்டம்

Congress ministers, now former ministers, languish in longing. However often they kneel to regain their lost posts, nothing has worked. When authority was in their hands they made a great display; now that it is gone, their dancing has subsided. Though the leaders are in this condition, their followers have not curbed their impudent tongues. The retinue's restlessness has not yet ceased. Even though the petty posts available to them contain no substance, they behave as if seated upon great thrones and continue their antics.

I do not say the former ministers' swollen pride has vanished completely. As the saying goes, something unfinished and something lingering remain. Gopala Reddi continues to leap about; the others mutter. Wherever Prakasam speaks, he laments about one thing or another. Dr Subbarayan's precise condition is difficult to state. For a while he may have little “fascination” with the political race, for a lively horse race is beginning at Guindy. Such is the ministers' condition. But the retinue that lived by trusting their pavilion has not stopped its crooked dance. Can an inborn nature disappear? Is the ability to understand one's actual position so ordinary? When even Rajaji, advertised as knowing everything, placed his foot without knowing the depth, need the conduct of the rest be explained? When the grinding stone and pestle themselves flew into the sky, could banyan and peepal leaves remain still?

Most municipal councils and district boards are in Congress hands. While members of their class held ministerial office, the retinue occupying posts in these bodies lived exuberantly. They had the confidence to ask, “Who is there to question us?” If caught in trouble, they believed that stroking Rajaji's sacred feet would obtain grace. Their habit was to call ministers and appeal over everything. Those ministers too behaved without knowing where they should or should not intervene.

When police filed cases against those who entered the Bharati meeting at Ettayapuram and created disorder, did they not have the cases withdrawn? Did Rajaji not grant refuge to the hooligans? Could there be a greater political obscenity? Has any minister anywhere intervened so openly to disrupt justice? If a minister advocates for hooligans in this fashion, need one ask why hooliganism grows? There is little surprise that the followers of such a leader remain restless. What charge can be made against them except that the disciples have surpassed the guru?

The courts held by these retinues in some places truly provoke laughter. They continue their performance without even realising that the world has awakened. One then sees how much audacity and ignorance overflow in them.

One Congress district-board president asked the clerks in the board office to hold a feast celebrating his victory. He did not stop there. This virtuous man reportedly prepared a schedule fixing how much each clerk must contribute according to salary and circulated it. When word of this amusement began leaking out and the street started laughing, the hero thought, “Our trick may be exposed; let us stop,” and cancelled the feast arranged through this punitive levy.

Why is there a vice-president when a district board has a president? If work or inconvenience prevents the president from conducting office business, the vice-president is appointed to perform it. But Congress comrades delight in throwing rules to the wind. When a district-board president contests an election, the proper course is to hand authority to the vice-president and withdraw; otherwise administration passes to an official.

The Congress president I refer to did neither. He handed power to neither vice-president nor senior official, but found a minor and inexperienced officer. Why? Others might not act according to his wishes. Give authority to an ordinary clerk, and gratitude will make him easy to manipulate. Such was the intention. So great is the desire to exercise power somehow. Otherwise the proverb that a petty man who gains prosperity holds an umbrella at midnight would become false!

Another petty figure! He is not a district-board president, but one step lower. In restlessness, however, he stands a step higher.

The moment he became a municipal chairman he imagined the world beneath his feet. He entered the office proposing reforms and economy. Yet every reform and economy consisted of dismissing poor non-Brahmin clerks. Even while performing an Aryan dance, would he lose sight of practical advantage? Economy or reform, the goal was employment for his caste fellows.

That is what Congress Iyers truly consider self-rule to mean. To our Tamil Congressmen, self-rule means breaking Mother India's chains, tying her loosened hair, shouting “Victory to Mother India!”, carrying flags, and throwing stones at an opponent's meeting. To the Iyers it means name, fame, money, office, and jobs for relatives and friends. Therefore they prosper while the Tamil suffers.

It is so not only in nationalism but religion. Which Brahmin rolls upon the road, wears a yellow dhoti and cries “Govinda! Govinda!” like our Tamil? Whatever guise they assume, their eyes remain fixed upon profit.

The municipal chairman mentioned above is an Iyer. The whole town now rumours that he has embarked upon a romantic escapade too. Yet does any Tamil Congressman even listen to it or condemn it in the slightest? If questioned, the Iyer will set the city Congress committee in motion; if that fails, he will go to the district, provincial, or All-India committee. Everywhere he has influence. His flag-bearing comrade can only sit at home and seethe. Can he leap? If he does, his remaining place is gone. He has seen what befell Subhas Bose, Nariman and Khare. Will he open his mouth? If he does, the Iyer will twist his ear.

Thus the romantic affair proceeds splendidly without restraint or concealment. The lady teaches poetry to the Iyer and lessons to his children, and all are cheerful. Had a Justice Party man done this, how much condemnation would arise? How many hotels would discuss it? How far would rumours be stretched? How many leaflets and wall pictures would appear? What suffering would be inflicted upon him? Because this man is an Iyer, he passes his days in petty mischief with no one to roar or question.

Whether the matter is great or small, do Brahmins possess the habit of exposing members of their own class? One day I heard a conversation with my own ears. One speaker was a Brahmin, the other a non-Brahmin. They discussed Sir C. P. Ramaswami Iyer, whose court then operated vigorously in Travancore while men and women entered prison. Both were Congressmen, speaking in a hotel. They did not know me, and I do not wish to name them. I mark the Brahmin as A and the non-Brahmin as D; you may understand those letters as Aryan and Dravidian if you wish.

**D:** Iyer, did you see today's newspaper?

**A:** Was there anything special?

**D:** Are you blind? C. P. is holding court in Travancore. Six were arrested yesterday, including two women. Did you not read it?

**A:** Oh, that! Will they not keep quiet?

**D:** What are you saying, Iyer? That Diwan is destroying Congress there. You too are a Congressman and committee president. My blood boils. What is the matter with you? Do you think one Iyer must never abandon another?

**A:** Enough, stop. Has the Erode smell reached you? What is wrong in C. P.'s action? Who would leave agitators unpunished? You plead for them, but what do you know of the matter? Those Congressmen there are tormenting C. P.'s life.

**D:** Ah, what tenderness! When we picketed and agitated and were imprisoned, how fiercely we abused officials and condemned them. Yet your mouth cannot speak of C. P.'s wrongdoing. What is wrong in what that Naicker says? Caste protects caste.

**A:** Restrain your tongue.

**D:** Be quiet; do not leap so much. What magnificent justice! However many wrongs a Brahmin commits, none is wrong. Is that not your argument?

**A:** You have become a pure communalist. At the next committee meeting, either you must leave or I shall resign. We shall see.

**D:** Go! Let your committee collapse. You need me, my elder brother and younger brother only when hunting votes. If I speak justice now, you expel me? Do it then.

**A:** Why should I expel you? I shall report the matter to the committee and let them decide. If a man who speaks communalism remains secretary, will the committee not become a ruined wall?

**D:** Let it become a ruined wall; let anything happen. I want no association with you.

When the non-Brahmin left the hotel in fury, I stopped him and obtained his address. I regularly sent him *Viduthalai* and *Kudi Arasu*. He is now firm and clear. He frequently advises me that Tamil associations should be established everywhere and gives whatever help he can.

Many among our Tamils are trapped and tormented by petty Congress courtiers of this kind.

Another worthy man is also a district-board president. During an election, schoolchildren did not shout victory slogans for him. How was that an offence? Giving various excuses, this petty deity expelled those children from school and reportedly harassed one or two teachers. See these petty antics! What greater shame is needed than that we entrust towns and districts to people of this nature and continue living?

Consider S. Satyamurti Iyer after becoming Mayor of Madras and see his display. He disliked the motorcar bought with corporation funds only last year and purchased a new one. The money belongs to the public. The corporation survives by taking loan upon loan, yet he imposes new expenses. There is to be a mayor's office, a separate establishment for it, and monthly salaries of 150 or 200 rupees.

Why this waste? Must ordinary people's money be squandered unnecessarily for his display? Is there no one to question it? Is there no one to curb the retinue's restlessness? Answer me! Have our people no sense of outrage?

*Kudi Arasu*, 24 December 1939

---NOTES---
`Parivaram` denotes a leader's attendants, followers or political retinue; “retinue” preserves the article's courtly sarcasm. `Former ministers` refers to Congress ministers who had left office in the political circumstances of 1939. `Rajaji` is C. Rajagopalachari. Gopala Reddi, T. Prakasam, Dr P. Subbarayan, Subhas Bose, K. F. Nariman, N. B. Khare, Sir C. P. Ramaswami Iyer and S. Satyamurti retain their familiar names.

`Erode smell` is a hostile reference within the reported dialogue to the Self-Respect movement and Periyar E. V. Ramasamy. `Naicker` in the same exchange refers to Periyar. *Viduthalai* and *Kudi Arasu* were movement publications. `Govinda` is a devotional cry.

The article contains caste-based polemic and allegations about identifiable public figures and unnamed local officials. They are translated as the author's claims and reported speech, not independently verified findings. Several local identities are deliberately withheld in the source and remain unnamed here.
